{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2252 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c82b19ce332adf56b916a9715446e6f2d23b997c1a51d58543edd15c26768e44" :edb/db-before-t 2251 :edb/db-after-t 2252 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535564 50 #inst "2026-10-09T02:36:57.976Z" 13194139535564 true] [17592186133609 1100 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\np_1(t)=t+1, \\qquad\np_2(t)=t, \\qquad\np_3(t)=t-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ of polynomials of degree $1$ or less in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle =p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p_1(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- $p_2(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- $S=\\{ p_1(t), p_2(t), p_3(t) \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535564 true] [17592186133609 1100 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n$p_1(t)=t+1, \\qquad p_2(t)=t, \\qquad p_3(t)=t-1$.\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ of polynomials of degree $1$ or less in the variable $t$ is defined as\n$\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle =p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1)$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $p_1(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $p_2(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S=\\{p_1(t), p_2(t), p_3(t) \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535564 false] [17592186133609 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n$⟨p_{1}(t), p_{3}(t)⟩|= ⟨t + 1, t - 1⟩; = (t + 1)\\begin{vmatrix}_{-1} \\cdot (t - 1) & _{-1} + (t + 1) & _{1} \\cdot (t - 1)\\end{vmatrix}_{1}; = (-1 + 1) \\cdot (-1 - 1) + (1 + 1) \\cdot (1 - 1); = 0$,\nwhich means that $p_1(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n- Statement II is false since\n$⟨p_{2}(t), p_{3}(t)⟩|= ⟨t, t - 1⟩; = (t)\\begin{vmatrix}_{-1} \\cdot (t - 1) & _{-1} + (t) & _{1} \\cdot (t - 1)\\end{vmatrix}_{1}; = (-1) \\cdot (-1 - 1) + (1) \\cdot (1 - 1); = 2 \\ne 0$,\nwhich means that $p_2(t) \\not\\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n- Statement III is false. Since $p_2(t) \\not\\perp p_3(t)$, the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535564 false] [17592186133609 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t+1,t-1⟩ \\\\  & =(t+1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{-1}+(t+1){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1+1)\\cdot (-1-1)+(1+1)\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $p_1(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,t-1⟩ \\\\  & =(t){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{-1}+(t){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1-1)+(1)\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $p_2(t) \\not\\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $p_2(t) \\not\\perp p_3(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535564 true] [17592186133610 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067643/assets/c817b41f0c393fcd49b34d4d7cb6ea6d4b9caf9aac7576c4c678090af3883c8f.png)\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_3$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- $S=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535564 true] [17592186133610 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as\n$\\langle \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_3$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S=\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535564 false] [17592186133610 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n$⟨v_{1}, v_{2}⟩|= 3(2)(-1) + (2)(1) + 2(1)(2); =-6 + 2 + 4; = 0$,\nwhich means that $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2$ with respect to the given inner product.\n- Statement II is false since\n$⟨v_{1}, v_{3}⟩|= 3(2)(0) + (2)(1) + 2(1)(1); = 0 + 2 + 2; = 4 \\ne 0$,\nwhich means that $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\not\\perp \\mathbf{v}_3$ with respect to the given inner product.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\not\\perp \\mathbf{v}_3$, the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535564 false] [17592186133610 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =3(2)(-1)+(2)(1)+2(1)(2) \\\\  & =-6+2+4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =3(2)(0)+(2)(1)+2(1)(1) \\\\  & =0+2+2 \\\\  & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\not\\perp \\mathbf{v}_3$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\not\\perp \\mathbf{v}_3,$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535564 true] [17592186133611 1100 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\nf_1(t)=1, \\qquad\nf_2(t)=t, \\qquad\nf_3(t)=2t+1\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathcal{C}[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\int_{-1}^{1} f(t)g(t) \\: \\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f_1(t) \\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- $f_2(t) \\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- $S=\\{ f_1(t), f_2(t), f_3(t) \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535564 true] [17592186133611 1100 "Consider the following set of functions:\n$f_1(t)=1, \\qquad f_2(t)=t, \\qquad f_3(t)=2t+1$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathcal{C}[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as\n$\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\int_{-1}^{1} f(t)g(t) \\: \\text{d}t$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_1(t) \\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $f_2(t) \\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S=\\{f_1(t), f_2(t), f_3(t) \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535564 false] [17592186133611 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n$⟨f_{1}(t), f_{3}(t)⟩|= ⟨1, 2t + 1⟩; = ∫_{-1}^{1}(2t + 1)dt; = (t^{2} + t) \\mid _{-1}^{1}; = 1^{2} + 1 - ((-1)^{2} + (-1)); = 2 \\ne 0$.\nThis means that $f_1(t) \\not\\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n- Statement II is false. We have\n$⟨f_{2}(t), f_{3}(t)⟩|= ⟨t, 2t + 1⟩; = ∫_{-1}^{1}t(2t + 1)dt; = ∫_{-1}^{1}(2t^{2} + t)dt; = \\frac{2t^{3}}{3} + \\frac{t^{2}}{2} \\mid _{-1}^{1}; = (2(1)^{3})/(3) + \\frac{1^{2}}{2} - ((2(-1)^{3})/(3) + ((-1)^{2})/(2)); = \\frac{7}{6} + \\frac{1}{6}; = \\frac{4}{3} \\ne 0$.\nThis means that $f_2(t) \\not\\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n- Statement III is false. Since $f_1(t) \\not\\perp f_2(t)$, the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139535564 false] [17592186133611 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,2t+1⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+1-((-1)^{2}+(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $f_1(t) \\not\\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,2t+1⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t(2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2{t}^{2}+t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-(\\frac{2(-1)^{3}}{3}+\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $f_2(t) \\not\\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f_1(t) \\not\\perp f_2(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139535564 true] [17592186133793 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355282 13194139535564 true] [17592186133793 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355289 13194139535564 true] [17592186133793 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355296 13194139535564 true] [17592186133793 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355303 13194139535564 true] [17592186133793 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355310 13194139535564 true] [17592186133794 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355331 13194139535564 true] [17592186133794 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355338 13194139535564 true] [17592186133794 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355359 13194139535564 true] [17592186133794 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355366 13194139535564 true] [17592186133795 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355317 13194139535564 true] [17592186133795 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355324 13194139535564 true] [17592186133795 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355345 13194139535564 true] [17592186133795 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355352 13194139535564 true] [17592186133795 1020 #edb/ref 17592186355373 13194139535564 true] [17592186134480 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535564 true] [17592186134480 1100 "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535564 true] [17592186134480 1100 "$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space $R^{3}$ is defined as\n$⟨x, y⟩ = x_{1}y_{1} + 2x_{2}y_{2} + 3x_{3}y_{3}$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v_{1}⊥v_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $v_{1}⊥v_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S = {v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535564 false] [17592186134480 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(6)(-1)+2(0)(1)+3(-2)(-1) \\\\  & =-6+0+6 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(6)(1)+2(0)(1)+3(-2)(0) \\\\  & =6+0+0 \\\\  & =6\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${v}_{1}⊥̸{v}_{3},$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355282 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355283 13194139535564 true] [17592186355282 1089 "q-100084" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355282 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535564 true] [17592186355282 1100 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2-2t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355282 1113 #uuid "9f1734bf-fa43-5318-a6a6-e6b722d892e3" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355282 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,2-2t⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{1}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(1-2)\\cdot (2-2(1))+(2-2)\\cdot (2-2(2)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,2t⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{1}\\cdot (2t){|}_{1}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(1-2)\\cdot (2(1))+(2-2)\\cdot (2(2)) \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355284 13194139535564 true] [17592186355283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355285 13194139535564 true] [17592186355283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355286 13194139535564 true] [17592186355283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355287 13194139535564 true] [17592186355283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355288 13194139535564 true] [17592186355283 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535564 true] [17592186355283 1041 "selection" 13194139535564 true] 