535564 true] [17592186355289 1113 #uuid "2017fb7c-732e-59a8-89fe-370ae0553d65" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355289 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-1,3t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-1){|}_{0}\\cdot (3t){|}_{0}+(2t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (3t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(2(0)-1)\\cdot (3(0))+(2(1)-1)\\cdot (3(1)) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨3t,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(3t){|}_{0}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{0}+(3t){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(3(0))\\cdot (1-0)+(3(1))\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355291 13194139535564 true] [17592186355290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355292 13194139535564 true] [17592186355290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355293 13194139535564 true] [17592186355290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355294 13194139535564 true] [17592186355290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355295 13194139535564 true] [17592186355290 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535564 true] [17592186355290 1041 "selection" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355290 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355295 13194139535564 true] [17592186355290 1110 #uuid "b110e252-d3d5-5cf4-ad84-e5701f514fbe" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355291 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355291 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535564 true] [17592186355291 1111 #uuid "752791d2-89a3-59cd-8bb5-aaf38615897d" 13194139535564 true] [1