rue. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+1,3t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+1){|}_{0}\\cdot (3t){|}_{0}+(2t+1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (3t){|}_{-1} \\\\  & =(2(0)+1)\\cdot (3(0))+(2(-1)+1)\\cdot (3(-1)) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨3t,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(3t){|}_{0}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{0}+(3t){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1} \\\\  & =(3(0))\\cdot (0+1)+(3(-1))\\cdot ((-1)+1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355298 13194139535564 true] [17592186355297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355299 13194139535564 true] [17592186355297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355300 13194139535564 true] [17592186355297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355301 13194139535564 true] [17592186355297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355302 13194139535564 true] [17592186355297 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535564 true] [17592186355297 1041 "selection" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355297 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355300 13194139535564 true] [17592186355297 1110 #uuid "5b255eaf-31fd-51ea-9587-17275622fc6a" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355298 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355298 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535564 true] [17592186355298 1111 #uuid "766e4695-fb97-5682-9e3b-3eb0935ff196" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355299 1034 "I only" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355299 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535564 true] [17592186355299 1111 #uuid "77fed978-502f-5574-891b-07e0ddc52543" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355300 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355300 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535564 true] [17592186355300 1111 #uuid "c8ae42b6-ab96-5144-9393-d9ab8d25976e" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355301 1034 "None of the statements is true" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355301 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535564 true] [17592186355301 1111 #uuid "90a76e68-8664-5cb2-94cd-7748eaad63be" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355302 1034 "II only" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355302 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535564 true] [17592186355302 1111 #uuid "aed8dc11-b380-5ee7-8625-4d672e9f005a" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355303 1017 #edb/ref 17592186355304 13194139535564 true] [17592186355303 1089 "q-104734" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355303 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535564 true] [17592186355303 1100 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}+t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $2$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355303 1113 #uuid "ee86e69a-f5bc-556f-81ec-284178e804fc" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355303 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}+t,{t}^{2}-t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,+\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,(-1))\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,+\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,+\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}+t,{t}^{2}-1⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{-1}\\,+\\,({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,+\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,+\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,+\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,+\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Let's check if ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-t,{t}^{2}-1⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,(-1))\\,\\cdot \\,((-1)^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, $S$ is an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355304 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355305 13194139535564 true] [17592186355304 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355306 13194139535564 true] [17592186355304 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355307 13194139535564 true] [17592186355304 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355308 13194139535564 true] [17592186355304 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355309 13194139535564 true] [17592186355304 1040 #edb/ref 1759