ct\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355310 1113 #uuid "491441f3-2dd7-5ce2-8d24-630838b4472c" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355310 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-4,{t}^{2}-t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2(2)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-t,{t}^{2}-2t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,0)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,2(0))\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,1)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,2(1))\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}-2(2)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2}-4,{t}^{2}-2t⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{0}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{1}+({t}^{2}\\,-\\,4){|}_{2}\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({0}^{2}\\,-\\,2(0))\\,+\\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({1}^{2}\\,-\\,2(1))\\,+\\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,4)\\,\\cdot \\,({2}^{2}\\,-\\,2(2)) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355311 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355312 13194139535564 true] [17592186355311 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355313 13194139535564 true] [17592186355311 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355314 13194139535564 true] [17592186355311 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355315 13194139535564 true] [17592186355311 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355316 13194139535564 true] [17592186355311 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535564 true] [17592186355311 1041 "selection" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355311 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355315 13194139535564 true] [17592186355311 1110 #uuid "086d5260-1618-5c6c-90f3-82bdb7b87a86" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355312 1034 "None of the statements are true" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355312 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535564 true] [17592186355312 1111 #uuid "f7894a5a-4cda-5d45-aa1c-173cf6d81be3" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355313 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355313 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535564 true] [17592186355313 1111 #uuid "43f02554-0e4f-57a5-9a30-321da9cc0e4d" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355314 1034 "I only" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355314 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535564 true] [17592186355314 1111 #uuid "88066b2e-a627-507f-9ef6-70ff53f0fa55" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355315 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355315 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535564 true] [17592186355315 1111 #uuid "dbc004e9-f1e8-5478-8ec5-6b0536db3994" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355316 1034 "II only" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355316 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13