,{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355373 1113 #uuid "c7ee428a-fc38-5401-8d0d-97e56021ccdf" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355373 1145 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{e}^{t},t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}(t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-t){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-2-(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{e}^{t},{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355375 13194139535564 true] [17592186355374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355376 13194139535564 true] [17592186355374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355377 13194139535564 true] [17592186355374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355378 13194139535564 true] [17592186355374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186355379 13194139535564 true] [17592186355374 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535564 true] [17592186355374 1041 "selection" 13194139535564 true] [17592186355374 1104 #edb/ref 17592186355377 13194139535564 true] [17592186355374 1110 #uuid "f56efe2b-8abb-559c-8002-1b2108a4dc1f" 13194139535564 true] [1759218635537